1
00:00:05,271 --> 00:00:10,257
This is Mara. She runs the depot at
Halstead Field Services, and her day starts the

2
00:00:10,257 --> 00:00:12,841
same way as everybody
else's: she signs in.

3
00:00:13,293 --> 00:00:18,260
Everything about her own hours lives on
one page. My shifts. No timesheet to fill

4
00:00:18,260 --> 00:00:21,141
in, no form to remember
at the end of the week.

5
00:00:21,595 --> 00:00:26,223
One button starts the shift. HBCField
reads her location, confirms she is at the

6
00:00:26,223 --> 00:00:30,966
depot, and records where she clocked in —
so nobody has to take anybody's word for

7
00:00:30,966 --> 00:00:31,487
it later.

8
00:00:31,938 --> 00:00:37,205
From here the clock runs by itself. She
can close the laptop, go out on a job, and

9
00:00:37,205 --> 00:00:38,811
the shift keeps counting.

10
00:00:39,263 --> 00:00:43,854
At the end of the day she clocks out.
The hours are worked out for her — time on

11
00:00:43,854 --> 00:00:46,322
site, breaks taken
off, ready for approval.

12
00:00:46,775 --> 00:00:51,541
Her own record is right there underneath
— every shift this month, with the time

13
00:00:51,541 --> 00:00:52,673
worked on each one.

14
00:00:53,126 --> 00:00:57,697
And because Mara supervises the depot,
she gets the other half: the whole team's

15
00:00:57,697 --> 00:01:01,810
attendance. Who is on the clock
right now, and how the day is adding up.

16
00:01:02,263 --> 00:01:07,177
Every shift is listed with the times, the
hours and the site. Nothing was typed in

17
00:01:07,177 --> 00:01:07,836
by anybody.

18
00:01:08,288 --> 00:01:13,384
Only the ones that need a decision are
flagged for her. A late start, a long day.

19
00:01:13,384 --> 00:01:16,090
Everything else has
already settled itself.

